  "live_records": {
    "q-143371": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143371",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14191704/assets/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*",
      "worked_solution": "Since $n=120$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.2,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.2=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.2)=96>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.2\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.2\\pm 0.060\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
