    "q-143307": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143307",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "A survey shows that $66$ out of a random sample of $220$ fans of a popular MLB baseball team are against the way their current coach has run the team.\nFind a $90%$ confidence interval for the proportion of the team's fans who are against the way the coach has run the team.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of fans of a certain MLB baseball team,\n\n- some of the fans are against the way their current coach has run the team, and some are not,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=220,$ and\n\n- the proportion of fans from the sample who are against the way the coach has run the team can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{66}{220}=0.3\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=220\\cdot 0.3=66>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=220\\cdot (1-0.3)=154>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14191704/assets/2ed94fdbb0eb4cc1dda3720592488546a02e157598e77a70fbd02d916906b56f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{220}} \\\\  & 0.3\\pm 0.051\\end{aligned}\n$$",
