    "q-142726": {
      "dom_id": "step-q142726",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "A survey of $150$ randomly selected students at a particular university showed that only $24$ select at least one math course. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the proportion of students from the entire university who select at least one math course. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of the students at a particular university,\n\n- some of the students select at least one math course, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=150,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who select at least one math course can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{24}{150}=0.16\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=150\\cdot 0.16=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=150\\cdot (1-0.16)=126>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14191704/assets/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.16\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.16(1-0.16)}{150}} \\\\  & 0.16\\pm 0.059\\end{aligned}\n$$",
